{# ===========================================================================
   Component: hdr

   THE site header. Included by layouts/base.njk, so every page renders this
   exact file: there is no second header variant that can drift from it.

   Two changes against the earlier four-item header, signed off 2026-08-03:
     1. Locations becomes a disclosure that expands to the seven rooms.
     2. Order Online joins the top level as the header's single red CTA.

   Everything in the panel comes from locations.json. North Park is absent
   because it is not in locations.json, which is the correct reason: it is
   closed, and nothing should have to remember to exclude it.

   THIRD CHANGE, 2026-09-17, replacing the scroll-responsive header this same
   audit tried and then scrapped: one state, no JavaScript sizing anything.
   Short by being dense rather than by hiding or shrinking the logo. The CTA
   is rendered here as a sibling of the logo and the nav, not inside the nav
   loop, so hdr.css can place it next to the logo on mobile's first row while
   the four plain nav items wrap to a second row beneath, full width. At
   desktop widths hdr.css puts it back inline with the rest of the nav; see
   the responsive block there for both layouts.

   ACCESSIBILITY
   The trigger is a real <button> with aria-expanded and aria-controls, not a
   link with a hover handler, so it works from the keyboard and announces its
   state. Escape closes and returns focus to the trigger. The panel is
   `hidden` when closed, so its links are out of the tab order entirely rather
   than merely invisible.

   EACH CARD RENDERS TWICE NOW, MOBILE AND DESKTOP, CSS-TOGGLED, 2026-09-18.
   The dense header rebuild (2026-09-17) made every card one link, name and
   neighborhood: the mobile audit had measured 1.5 of the old photo cards
   visible in the panel at 390px, and this panel exists to answer one
   question fast, "which of these seven is mine". Right call for a phone.
   The bug was landing it unscoped: the rewrite deleted the photo,
   descriptor, live-status, Directions and Order elements outright instead of
   confining the simplification to mobile, so 1280-1440px lost all of it too,
   the same four-across compact link a phone gets.

   Fixed by keeping both. `.loc-card` is the mobile link exactly as the
   rebuild built it, one link, two lines. `.loc-card-full` is the pre-rebuild
   card restored verbatim under its own block name so nothing collides with
   `.loc-card`'s classes, with the room's own page one tap further for
   everything it doesn't repeat. mega-menu.css shows one or the other by
   width, at 48rem, the breakpoint the panel itself already uses to switch
   from a floating dropdown to a mobile accordion.
#}

{#
  `megaInstance` disambiguates the ids when the header is rendered more than
  once in one document, which /nav-preview/ does (interactive at the top, and
  again forced open below). Without it both copies claim
  id="mega-locations-trigger", the ids are no longer unique, and aria-controls
  and aria-labelledby resolve to whichever the parser saw first. Real pages
  render the header once and never set this.
#}
{%- set megaId = "mega-" + (megaInstance | default("")) %}
{%- set ctaItem = (nav.primary | where("cta", true)) | first %}
<header class="hdr hdr--mega">
  <div class="hdr__in">
    {#
      Two assets, one per theme, swapped by CSS in hdr.css. NEVER a filter: a
      filter-based fake was shipped in an earlier draft and turned the mark
      pink. Both files are horizontal lockups at 2.25:1 and 2.34:1, so the swap
      costs about 4% of width and no layout shift.

      alt lives on one image only. Both are in the DOM at all times and CSS
      hides the inactive one, so two alt strings would announce the brand name
      twice. The hidden one is decorative by definition.

      THE ONLY LOGO TREATMENT. No smaller mark, no icon-only variant, no swap
      by scroll position: the full wordmark lockup, this same size, at every
      scroll position and every viewport this header ships at.
    #}
    <a class="hdr__logo" href="/" aria-label="{{ site.name }} home">
      <img
        class="hdr__logo-img hdr__logo-dark"
        src="/images/brand/tajima-logo-header-dark.png"
        alt="{{ site.logo.alt }}"
        width="800"
        height="356"
        fetchpriority="high">
      <img
        class="hdr__logo-img hdr__logo-light"
        src="/images/brand/tajima-logo-header-light.png"
        alt=""
        width="800"
        height="342"
        fetchpriority="high">

    </a>

    {%- if ctaItem %}
    {#- Rendered here, not inside the nav loop below: see the THIRD CHANGE
        note above for why, and hdr.css for how it lands next to the logo on
        mobile and back inline with the rest of the nav at desktop widths. -#}
    <a
      class="hdr__nav-link hdr__nav-link--cta"
      href="{{ ctaItem.url }}"
      {%- if page.url == ctaItem.url %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>
      {{ ctaItem.label }}
    </a>
    {%- endif %}

    <nav class="hdr__nav" aria-label="Primary">
      {%- for item in nav.primary | nonEmpty("nav.primary") %}
      {#- The CTA item renders once already, above, next to the logo. Skipping
          it here rather than filtering nav.primary itself keeps this loop
          reading exactly like the data it walks. -#}
      {%- if not item.cta %}
        {%- set isCurrent = page.url == item.url %}

        {%- if item.mega %}
        {# ---------------- Locations, the disclosure ---------------- #}
        <div class="mega" data-mega>
          <button
            type="button"
            class="hdr__nav-link mega__trigger{% if isCurrent %} hdr__nav-link--current{% endif %}"
            id="{{ megaId }}{{ item.mega }}-trigger"
            aria-expanded="false"
            aria-controls="{{ megaId }}{{ item.mega }}-panel"
            data-mega-trigger>
            {{ item.label }}
            <svg class="mega__chevron" width="10" height="7" viewBox="0 0 10 7" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
              <path d="M1 1.5 5 5.5 9 1.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
            </svg>
          </button>

          <div
            class="mega__panel"
            id="{{ megaId }}{{ item.mega }}-panel"
            role="group"
            aria-labelledby="{{ megaId }}{{ item.mega }}-trigger"
            data-mega-panel
            hidden>
            <div class="mega__inner">

              <p class="mega__lede">
                Six rooms across San Diego, one in Kihei. Walk in, no reservations.
              </p>

              <ul class="mega__grid">
                {%- for loc in locations.items %}
                {#
                  `placeLine` matches the collision rule the full location
                  pages already use: Crown Point's neighborhood IS "Crown
                  Point", so printing loc.neighborhood there would repeat the
                  word. Where they collide, the locality carries the line
                  instead. Same rule Maui's plate uses.
                #}
                {%- set placeLine = loc.neighborhood if (loc.neighborhood and loc.neighborhood != loc.name) else loc.address.locality %}
                <li class="mega__cell">
                  {#- THE MOBILE CARD, unchanged since the dense header rebuild
                      (2026-09-17): one link, two lines, name and neighborhood.
                      Correct for a panel that has to show seven of these
                      without scrolling on a phone. Hidden at desktop widths by
                      mega-menu.css; see .loc-card-full below for what desktop
                      shows instead. -#}
                  <a class="loc-card" href="{{ loc.url }}">
                    <span class="loc-card__name">{{ loc.name }}</span>
                    <span class="loc-card__place">{{ placeLine }}</span>
                  </a>

                  {#- THE DESKTOP CARD, restored 2026-09-18. The dense header
                      rebuild deleted this entirely rather than scoping it to
                      desktop, which is the bug: a floating dropdown at
                      1280-1440px showed the same two-line mobile link, four
                      across, with no photo, descriptor, live status,
                      Directions or Order button. This is that markup, ported
                      back from the commit before the rebuild (2566263^) with
                      a distinct loc-card-full block name throughout so
                      nothing here can collide with .loc-card/.loc-card__name/
                      .loc-card__place above, which stay exactly as built for
                      mobile. mega-menu.css hides this block below 48rem, the
                      same breakpoint the panel itself already uses to switch
                      from a floating dropdown to a mobile accordion (not the
                      36rem the header chrome switches at): a rich card in a
                      narrow accordion column would be the reverse of this
                      bug, desktop styling at mobile width. -#}
                  <article class="loc-card-full">
                    <a class="loc-card-full__link" href="{{ loc.url }}">
                      {#- Same placeholder photo policy as before the rebuild:
                          one real Tajima photograph (Karaage, served at all
                          seven, menu.json-confirmed) standing in for the
                          rooms with no photography of their own yet. See
                          roomPhotos._placeholder. loading="lazy" matters:
                          this panel ships hidden, so nothing fetches until
                          someone opens it. -#}
                      {%- set cardPhoto = roomPhotos[loc.id].hero or roomPhotos._placeholder %}
                      <span class="loc-card-full__media" aria-hidden="true">
                        <img
                          class="loc-card-full__media-img"
                          src="{{ cardPhoto.src }}"
                          alt=""
                          loading="lazy"
                          decoding="async"
                          width="{{ cardPhoto.width }}"
                          height="{{ cardPhoto.height }}">
                        <span class="loc-card-full__media-no">{{ "0" if loop.index < 10 }}{{ loop.index }}</span>
                      </span>
                      <span class="loc-card-full__name">{{ loc.name }}</span>
                    </a>

                    {#- Suppressed where it would repeat placeLine, same rule
                        Maui's plate uses (its descriptor and place are both
                        "Kihei"). -#}
                    {%- if loc.descriptor and loc.descriptor != placeLine %}
                    <p class="loc-card-full__desc">{{ loc.descriptor }}</p>
                    {%- endif %}

                    {%- if loc.hours and loc.hours.length %}
                    <span class="loc-card-full__status" data-hours-status data-hours='{{ loc.hours | dump }}' hidden></span>
                    {%- elif loc.closesAt %}
                    {#- Maui: a closing time, not a live status. No hours
                        array, so no dot (the dot on the other six means
                        "computed from today's window"). -#}
                    <span class="loc-card-full__status loc-card-full__status--static">Closes {{ loc.closesAt | clockTime }}</span>
                    {%- endif %}

                    <p class="loc-card-full__hood">
                      <span class="loc-card-full__place">{{ placeLine }}</span>
                      <a class="link-quiet" href="{{ loc | mapsUrl }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                        Directions<span class="u-visually-hidden"> to {{ loc.name }}, opens in a new tab</span>
                        <svg width="9" height="9" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                          <path d="M5 1.5h7.5V9M12.5 1.5 5.5 8.5M9 12.5H1.5V5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                        </svg>
                      </a>
                    </p>

                    <a class="loc-card-full__order" href="{{ loc.orderUrl }}" rel="noopener">
                      Order online<span class="u-visually-hidden"> from {{ loc.name }}</span>
                    </a>
                  </article>
                </li>
                {%- endfor %}
              </ul>

              {#
                TIJUANA IS A LINE AND AN OUTBOUND LINK, NEVER AN EIGHTH CARD.
                Added 2026-09-14 on Steve's instruction, in the same treatment
                /locations/ already uses for it, and for the same reasons:
                SITE_ARCHITECTURE.md says "Tijuana: outbound link to
                tajimamx.com. Not a card", CLIENT_FACTS.md has the Tijuana
                operating count unresolved (older materials reference up to
                four), and voice-tone.md forbids publishing a global total. A
                card in this grid would number it and assert a count.

                tajimamx.com is a separate property on a separate domain, in
                Spanish, so this leaves the site: rel="external noopener" and
                an out-of-tab cue, the same as the Directions links above.
              #}
              <p class="mega__foot">
                <a class="mega__foot-link" href="{{ item.url }}">All locations, with addresses and phone numbers</a>
                <span class="mega__foot-aside">
                  South of the border?
                  <a class="mega__foot-link mega__foot-link--out" href="https://tajimamx.com" rel="external noopener" target="_blank">
                    Visit Tajima Tijuana<span class="u-visually-hidden">, opens tajimamx.com in a new tab</span>
                    <svg width="9" height="9" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                      <path d="M5 1.5h7.5V9M12.5 1.5 5.5 8.5M9 12.5H1.5V5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                    </svg>
                  </a>
                </span>
              </p>

            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        {%- else %}
        <a
          class="hdr__nav-link{% if item.sub %} hdr__nav-link--sub{% endif %}{% if isCurrent %} hdr__nav-link--current{% endif %}"
          href="{{ item.url }}"
          {%- if isCurrent %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>
          {{- item.label }}{% if item.sub %} <span aria-hidden="true">&#8629;</span>{% endif -%}
        </a>
        {%- endif %}
      {%- endif %}

      {%- endfor %}
    </nav>
  </div>
</header>
